A||N||12||RiceBurner515||Water pump pulley/bolts?||||8||
Z||000000||RiceBurner515||11-05-2000||07:59 PM||||how do i get the pulley connected to the water pump off without taking off my water pump and the whole bracket? im trying to take off my water pump but i can't get at one of the last remaining bolts right behind this pulley because its too small of a space to get at with a socket wrench and i can't get a wrench around the bolt anyway because of the angles.....what do i do, can i get the pulley off or is there some type of tool i oculd use to get down in there? ||||reg||8
Z||000001||wilburt||11-05-2000||08:22 PM||||You must have an '84 2.5, as I think '84 was only one with water pump pully bolts. I used an '85 pump and pully on an '84, 'cause I couldn't locate an '84 pump. However, if you take the battery out, and plastic battery shield, you should be able to access the bolts pretty easy with a 10mm open-end wrench. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RiceBurner515||11-05-2000||08:27 PM||||there aren't bolts on the pulley itself, the water pump bolt i need to get to is behind the pulley with about an inch of room down inbetween the pulley and bolt on the waterpump, i have a 85' SE.||||reg||1
Z||000003||wilburt||11-05-2000||08:36 PM||||Oh man, I after re-reading your post, I now understand what you are asking. Sorry!<P>You'll need a 3-arm puller to get the pully off the pump. I also tried to get a pump off with the pully attached. Hard to get at a couple of the bolts. What I did was get a 13mm socket, and clamp a vice-grip (locking pliers), on it really tight. Wiggle it behind the pully, and it'll fit.||||reg||11
Z||000004||Bob Englert||11-05-2000||08:52 PM||||When I changed the water pump on my daughter's 87 coupe 4 cyl I had the same problem.  I ended up removing the bracket the pump is mounted to from the block.  It was held by 4 bolts that are facing the firewall, one is very hard to see.  With the pump and bracket off the car I was able to remove the pulley with a power steering pump pulley puller I borrowed from AutoZone.  Then the pump bolts were easy to get to.<P>If you do this NAPA sells the gasket you will need when reinstalling.<P>Good luck...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000005||RiceBurner515||11-05-2000||08:52 PM||||what exactly is a 3 arm puller and how much do they go for?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||RiceBurner515||11-05-2000||08:55 PM||||a gasket for what? the bracket? i have one for the pump already.....????||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||11-05-2000||08:57 PM||||When I do those I do part from the top, part from the bottom and the others through the wheel well. If you have ratcheting box wrenches it helps tons, especially the low movement type.||||reg||1
Z||000008||wilburt||11-05-2000||09:00 PM||||Bob E's talking about the gasket that goes between the pump housing, and engine block. If you take the whole pump housing off, as Bob explains, you'll need this gasket. Bob is also right about Autozone, they'll let you borrow the correct puller.||||reg||1
Z||000009||prof bobo||11-06-2000||05:02 PM||||I had a similar problem with a power steering pump on a 4.3L in a Jimmy.  I drilled two holes in the front of the pulley just large enough for a socket to fit through to get to the mounting bolt, (two holes? one on each side to help keep the pulley from becoming unbalanced, see?).  Remove your rear wheel and rubber/plastic splash-skirt thingy to see if you have enough space to access and do something like this, eh? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||mbramble||11-06-2000||05:15 PM||||I had a similar problem when pulling the water pump from my 87 2.5L.  I ended up borrowing the puller from AutoZone and removed the pulley while it was still in the car.  Replaced the water pump and then re-installed the pulley in the car.  Was quite easy once it was figured out what was needed.||||reg||1
Z||000011||RiceBurner515||11-07-2000||03:06 PM||||with the power steering pumper pulley puller what exactly do i do and how does it work? do i just pull it off or something? i mean, i thought the pulley at the end of the water pump had threads in it?||||reg||1
Z||000012||mbramble||11-07-2000||06:08 PM||||It's a bit harder to explain than it would be for the Auto Zone guy to show you.  Anyway, the ps pulley puller has a bolt on which mounts two mating halves of the puller part.  They fit over the lip on the outside of the pulley and a nut threaded onto the bolt.  Once in place a metal band goes around the two halves to keep them from coming apart.  One end of the bolt goes into the end of the pulley shaft and the other has a hex head on it that you tighten.  Cranking on the hex head causes the puller to pull the pulley off against the bolt that is going into the pulley shaft.<P>Clear as mud, right?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 11-07-2000).]||||reg||1
